WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
Othello, a minute to learn, a lifetime to master.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:27.800
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.760
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.360
And I'm Joe. And that's it.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:40.860
That's it. But, you know, we should probably kick one of us off the show today because Othello is really a two-player game.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.820
Alright, I'll talk to you guys next week.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.680
No, no, no, I'll go.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.580
I actually like Othello.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.740
Yeah, a lot of eager people to get off the show very quickly.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.560
I love Othello. Do you want to talk about your love for Othello, Joe?

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.180
Sure, I'll give a little background.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:05.820
I'm not a fanatic, but I started playing Othello just casually as I would play Checkers, and I always liked it.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.580
I really don't have too much more to say about my love for Othello.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.040
It's not like I went to Othello tournaments or did any crazy story.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.440
But it's a fun strategy game.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.340
Like, not the NES game, the actual board game of Othello.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.220
Did you call it Othello or did you call it Reversi?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.960
I only heard about Reversi a couple minutes before we started recording.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.080
Mike mentioned that.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.720
I've only ever called it Othello.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:27.880
That's so weird.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.880
I mean, I had seen this game.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.500
Like, I'd seen people play it.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.540
I never really knew the rules.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.420
First, I thought it was just go, but I know it's not go.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.380
But the only other thing I had heard of was Reversi.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.700
I had never heard this described as Othello.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:53.180
Well, Sean, you gave me a great segue there because I also only knew it as Reversi until I started, like, I guess, looking into the game a little more.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.720
And then it became Othello, and that's how I knew it going into this episode.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:03.400
But the Othello thing actually comes because that's, like, that's the change between...

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.100
There is actual differences between Reversi and Othello.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:12.400
Yeah. And there are, like, really small differences, but apparently, like, Othello is a game that's, like, patented in Japan.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:19.200
And so, when you go to, like, the App Store, nobody can just, like, make a generic Othello game.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.520
You have to call it Reversi because nobody has, like, a license on that one.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.940
But you can't, like, if you go to the App Store and look up, like, I just want to play some Othello today, they'll just show you Reversi games instead.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.520
It's really weird that it's just the naming convention, I guess.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.020
Yeah, like, everything else about it is up for grabs.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:47.360
Yeah. The modern version of the game, Othello, if you will, we'll refer to it just as that going forward, is...

00:02:47.360 --> 00:03:00.440
The only difference is that in Reversi, when a player could not make a move, that was, like, the end of the game.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:06.680
And I believe now in Othello, when the player cannot make a move, then the next player you just pass.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.560
And the other player is just able to continue to go.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.460
Should we do, like, a quick overview of the rules of the game Othello?

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.920
Yeah, I think so.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:23.380
I think that's warranted because I imagine if I had to, like, rank the most common tabletop board game...

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:31.440
I don't even know if you call them board games, but tabletop games in the world, chess would probably be, like, number one, go number two, checkers number three.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.000
And Othello is, like, probably one that doesn't have as much exposure as anywhere near those other three.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.900
It's, like, somewhere in the range of, like, a mousetrap.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.220
Oh, everyone knows mousetrap.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.720
Everyone knows how to build a mousetrap.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:47.900
Nobody knows how to play it.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.560
Joe, you volunteered.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.540
Would you like to walk us through a little rendition?

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.180
You don't have to give me all the rules and all the complicatedness.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.800
I mean, it's pretty simple, really.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:03.140
You're on a board, I think it's an 8x8 board of 8x8 tiles, and there are...

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.100
You have little discs that are black on one side, white on the other.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.860
So you put them in a little checkerboard, like, square in the middle, just four discs,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:21.800
and then really you just place one of your discs in your color surrounding or, like, on either end of however many of the other player's color.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.580
So, like, if you bookend your other player's color, you can flip over any...

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:32.420
If you're white and you bookend the black pieces, you can flip over any black pieces in between your two white pieces.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.000
And that's really the game.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.580
The whole goal of the game is that once the board's filled, you want to have more of your pieces than the other player.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.640
And the result is actually kind of more complicated than it sounds because...

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:47.700
Oh, yeah.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.260
At any given moment, like, half the board can switch.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:05:02.260
Yeah, and another quick note is that you may only place something down if it will result in a flip.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.920
Like, you can't just sort of...

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.640
I'll put one over here that's not really interacting with the rest of the game.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.440
It just has to make a flip.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:20.860
Right, right. Yeah, and what's interesting here is, like, when Reverse first, like, became a thing, that was 1883.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:28.520
So, this is definitely the oldest video game, like, adaptation we're getting because of the time it took to get to here.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.920
But that's even, like, six years before Nintendo, the company, has found it.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:42.840
So, like, for Nintendo, this is kind of a big deal because Othello is really, like, a Japanese, you know, game.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.440
It was padded in Japan in 1971.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.160
They brought out the rulebook.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:51.260
They popularized the game a lot and opened up, like, official Othello tournaments.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:06:00.760
So, it does make sense that we're seeing this game before, say, like, you know, chess or checkers, which you would imagine you'd get way before you ever get a game like Othello.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:10.820
But I think that happens strictly because of the Japanese tie-in, and it's probably likely, although I didn't look it up, that this game came out in Japan long before...

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.800
On the Famicom long before it came to the NES.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.100
I buy that.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.580
Yeah. I didn't know that this game had its roots in Japan.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.900
I thought it was another...

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.040
I mean, I guess just because of the Reversi thing.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.400
But I guess that is, like, a European thing.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.880
Yes. Reversi is, I think, actually...

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.060
Is that the UK, I think, that's where that started?

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.940
I think it was in Scotland or Wales or something.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:40.660
Got it. And there's, like, two guys that each claim they made the game, and they say the other one's a liar.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.640
So, that is what it is.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.140
We'll never know the truth because it's been so long.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.120
Who could find the official documents?

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.220
But, Joe, I think you gave a really solid explanation of how to play this game.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:07:01.280
So, were you surprised at all, upon booting up the NES version of this game, that it really is just Othello?

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.400
Or is that about what we all expected to be doing?

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.960
I mean, I think that is what we expected.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:08.920
Or what I expected, rather.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.720
But, Mike, I mentioned to you earlier that, like, I really don't have too much...

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:14.980
Too much. I have a little bit.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.200
But to say about the NES game, because it's just...

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.400
Everything I have to say about the NES game is the stuff I have to say about Othello.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.580
Because it is pretty darn accurately just exactly Othello.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.160
I mean, it's not like any of the sports games we play where, like, they can't get it all...

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.820
The rules are simple enough where they can get it all into an NES game.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.640
So, no, I wasn't surprised.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:35.800
Sean, how about you?

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:49.280
Yeah, I mean, I guess the only things that you can say about its port from Meatspace to the NES is how does it handle...

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.580
How you interact with the game and how does it handle how you set up a game.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.000
I mean, I guess, like, you know, it's easier to set up.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.280
Um, uh, the fact that you have computer players is cool.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:08.200
They seem to be well-designed, um, in terms of their AI.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:15.140
Uh, and that's really all that you can say about the game outside of the actual board game, I think.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.000
And, like, it's got a very Spartan aesthetic.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.960
Like, they're not really doing too much to spice the game up.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.420
But I don't think it needs to.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:34.500
You must be reading my show notes because those two bullet points of how you interact with the game and computer difficulty are the two big talking points on my show notes today.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.380
Yeah. And so I'd love to...

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.380
So all you can talk about. Well, you'd be surprised.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:39.700
I will go on a rant later.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.860
I can't wait. But let's talk about how you interact with the game.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.040
Because I think that is the most important part of this whole thing, right?

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.120
Is that video games are the future, right?

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.520
In the future, all games will be played on your TV or now in VR or whatever.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.220
And, like, there will be no need for physical objects.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:03.460
But is a game like Othello where the pieces really just switch between black and white?

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:15.120
Like, is that a game that's complicated enough to warrant, like, oh, this will be so much better when I have the video game version because I won't have to waste my time flipping these tiles and hoping I made the right flips.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.000
Like, oh, I accidentally missed one this time.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:20.900
Is this a game that warrants, like, needing this much?

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.780
Like, you think about, like, Monopoly, right?

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.020
A real board game, which is not a real board game, of course, because we know what real board games are.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.100
But to the general population, real board games...

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.540
Nobody understands what you mean.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.780
We're like elitists of board games.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.220
Yeah, yeah. Real board games.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.780
Your Euro board game dominance.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.620
Or I'm sure, you know, Settlers of Catan is, like, now a big thing, right?

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:41.580
In, like, mainstream.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:47.980
And I think people would say that Settlers of Catan is way above Monopoly in terms of, like, a board game.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:53.600
Sure. You know? But Monopoly is a game that almost every American is familiar with.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.760
And that's a game that I believe we're going to get on the NES.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.960
But that's one that, like, makes sense, right?

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.660
There's a lot of, like, can you even trust the banker?

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.040
But then the banker has, like, a lot of different mistakes.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.400
And there's money to be handed out and properties.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.900
And you have to, like, keep it all in a tiny space to yourself.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.760
So, like, that makes sense.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:19.680
In a game where all you have to do is just pick up a pebble and put it on an 8x8 grid, do you need this to be on the NES?

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:28.960
Yes. I guess the one thing that does, Sean, you mentioned it that I forgot to mention when I was talking about it, is the AI.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:36.260
I mean, I guess in the 80s, you can't just, like, go online and play Othello online and you want to play Othello and you don't have someone to play it with.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.660
I guess that's a selling point that you can play against the computer.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.540
You can practice.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:42.560
You can hone your skills.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:53.440
But, yeah, to your point, I mean, if you were to just take Monopoly and Othello and forget about which one you like better and just decide based on, like, which one would have more value in being a video game, I guess Monopoly.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.740
I mean, there's more setup to be done.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.080
There's more that's going on.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.140
Whereas, like, Othello, yeah, you don't have to set up the board, but you're tied to your TV.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.940
So, like, setting up the board doesn't take that much time anyways.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:14.880
I, yeah, I think that the main draw for something like this is the fact that there are AI opponents.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:22.300
Like, there are computer opponents of varying difficulty with different rules that you can enforce.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.180
It is funny that, like, it's such a basic thing that we're simulating here.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:40.420
It almost feels like some of the first video games, which were just, like, just experiments of, like, well, let's just try and recreate this with machine code.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:47.200
Obviously, all you need is a piece of paper to play X's and O's.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:48.420
What was it? Oh, Jesus Christ.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:52.520
Tic-tac-toe. Rock over scissors.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:58.120
Obviously, all you need is a piece of paper to play that game, but let's have a computer do it.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:04.860
This almost feels like this kind of experiment, but no, I mean, just because it has a computer opponent, I think it has some value.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.060
Yeah, okay. I think that's a good point.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:14.080
And to be fair, like, anything that can be emulated into a video game experience is totally, like, justified to do so.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:16.660
Like, I'm not saying this was a waste of time.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:31.080
I'm arguing that I would just rather, having played this NES version, you know, whether Othello, like, itself is a great game or something like that, isn't, like, going to be changed on someone's opinion of, like, this port of it to the NES.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:44.420
And so I would actually say that in this case, because it is a pretty simple to set up game and play, it's kind of just more fun and immersive, if you will, to just play in the real world, like, physical.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:53.180
Just have, like, the tabletop game set up and flip those pieces, because the total time of that game versus an NES game is really not that much different.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:55.280
I totally understand where you guys are coming from.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:03.760
No, yeah. I mean, I'd very much prefer the tactile experience of playing the board game.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.160
But I just thought it was funny the way that you said that with the immersion.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:14.780
Like, whenever you go out into the baseball field and you're playing with your buddies, like, oh, this is so immersive.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:24.940
Right, right. Well, I can't be the only one of the group that actually sees, like, the war qualities of a game like Othello,

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:33.760
where it is actually, like, you know, there's a battlefield here and being defensive, you know, if we're talking strategies,

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:41.140
being defensive early on and then going on the attack later is, like, this beautiful art form in playing the game

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:51.120
where you kind of need to, like, focus on just maintaining ground at first and then expanding your ground later on when there's less choices to be had.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.780
So this way you keep that lead.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:57.340
I think that there is definitely, like, some war games going on here.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:05.320
And so, you know, I'm not saying that that's, like, immersive, but it's fun to, like, see that happen in front of you rather than with, you know, 8-bit graphics.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:07.640
That's true. I get what you're saying.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:11.920
But also to that point, then, Othello is such a...

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:17.560
As simple as it is, like, the more you play it, the more you realize that the strategy is, like, really, really deep in Othello.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.480
So, like, to that point, if you are, like, trying to get better at it and stuff,

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:28.000
then there's also just something really cool about being able to play against the computer over and over again,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:33.200
try out your different strategies, test them out, and, like, when there's no stakes of playing against an actual opponent.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:35.060
Granted, I mean, the computer might...

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:37.840
The AI might be flawed, but so would your opponent be.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:43.440
But, yeah, there's a lot of strategies that, like, you might think, well, would this work?

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:48.300
And it's just hard to get your mind around, so it's good to have that, like, model that you can test.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.140
So this game is more utility.

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.680
Yeah. Yeah, it's like a training center.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:58.940
Yeah. Yeah, that's a great point, though, Sean, about being a training center,

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:05.320
because I imagine that that's actually a very valid purpose for this kind of thing.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:14.100
You know, even today, but, like, back then, you know, computer chess, computer Go, computer Othello,

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:20.200
like, these high-strategy games that were, like, you know, oh, the best players in the world are, like,

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:25.620
so much better than the rest of, you know, the players that play is a really neat concept,

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:29.200
and to see it at, like, a high level, like Joe was saying, it is a very deep game.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:35.280
And when computers first started, it was actually, like, a programming challenge to say, like,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:41.920
how am I going to beat the best human players in the world using a computer, using mathematics,

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:48.400
and, you know, like, puzzles to solve this, and schedules and stuff like that?

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:50.180
Like, how am I going to overcome that?

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:55.780
And I think that that's where the NES version really shines, is that, you know,

00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:01.860
there's enough programming power here to really challenge the average player.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:07.260
I don't know about top players, but this could be a very challenging experience compared to,

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:13.120
you know, playing your friend in a casual two-player match where neither one of you really cares all that much about strategy.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:23.800
It's funny because in the manual for this game, it describes the different computer difficulty levels,

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:30.940
not so much in how much better they are in just, like, how they're going to make the decisions,

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:35.460
but it's more that the computer gets more time to think about the decision.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:40.440
Like, it's just, it's simulating however many permutations that this board can go,

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:46.480
and when it gets to the harder level, it stops, it doesn't cut it off so quickly,

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.580
so it makes the less than ideal move.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:56.540
So this is probably the most resource-intensive NES game we've played, if that's to be believed,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:02.820
because it's probably, it's probably doing all of that, like, at the highest clock speed that it can.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.660
So, yeah, no, I mean, I really like the history of computer chess,

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:14.620
and while it seems like Othello isn't as high permutation, maybe I'm wrong,

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.640
it's sort of the same concept.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:21.640
So, I actually have a question about that, because I wasn't sure what was happening

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:24.320
when you got into higher difficulties and it was taking longer.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:30.200
And I thought maybe it was, like, trying to make me feel like it's taking longer in thinking,

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:33.200
but do you think that it really, like, I don't know anything about computer chess and how that works,

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:37.760
but do you think it really was, like, taking that time in order to, like,

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:42.440
I just didn't think the AI would even be that advanced on the NES to be able to do that,

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:46.740
so I thought it was just, like, an immersion thing, which I thought would have been silly,

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:50.440
but if it is actually taking that time, that's really fascinating,

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:54.700
because it really is making the difficulty higher by just making the opponent better.

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.120
Go ahead, Sean. No, you can go ahead.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.360
Okay. Yeah, absolutely, Joe, because even, like, very early computers,

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:03.840
essentially, what I was calling schedules, I'm not sure where I came up with that,

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:10.320
I'm referring to, like, trees, where there's a bunch of, like, legal moves laid out in patterns

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:15.660
that computers recognize, and they look at the legal moves available to them,

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.200
and they're able to, like, look forward.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.700
Yeah. So, like, there's all these different trees, essentially,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.740
and they make deterministic things based on, like, the final outcome that they can see.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:29.060
Obviously, like, humans are not really capable of doing something like that.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:32.980
We're very bad at that. We're looking ahead that, you know, that many steps.

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:34.220
But a computer can.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:39.600
And obviously, like, in a game, you were saying this is an 8x8 board, which it is.

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:44.360
There are smaller Othello boards, but this is the one that you get access to in the NES version.

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:52.440
There's no way that the NES had enough power to do, like, 10 to the 54th power amount of trees,

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.060
you know, to figure out, like, all the different nodes possible to solve the game.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.580
I don't even think Othello's ever been solved, actually.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:01.740
I think it's still to this day not a solved game.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.260
Yeah. Wow. I mean, think about that number, though.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.780
10 to the 54th power.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:12.720
They've been able to get a computer Go that can beat a human, but...

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:13.680
No, no, no, sorry, no.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:15.100
Beat a human, no problem.

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:18.740
Othello can easily beat a human with today's computers.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:27.900
Oh, okay. It's not a solved game in the sense of, like, if you do all these moves, white can never win if you go first, you know?

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:31.340
Oh, interesting. Like, it has not been...

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.420
Where checkers has been solved, apparently.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.480
Okay. I mean, checkers is, yes, pretty simple.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:49.880
Yeah, yeah. But I'm saying, like, you know, that's the difference, I guess, is, like, you know, these computers try to brute force their way into, like, figuring out the one, like, series of steps that will always guarantee victory.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:57.160
But because there's so many possible outcomes, you know, computers even have a long way to go before we can figure out that answer.

00:19:57.400 --> 00:19:59.000
I didn't realize this was on that level, yeah.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:13.980
Yeah, to make a long story short, for Joe, the computer is absolutely doing that here, and that's the difference, is, like, the time that it gets to calculate is the time that it's able to, like, look ahead and make the best possible decision based on future moves.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:20.940
So when you're playing on difficulty one, it's not that the computer is, you know, like, set to be terrible and make dumb moves.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.320
I don't think computer difficulty one ever really makes a stupid move.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:33.200
No. But I think it might make a suboptimal move compared to the level four, which I just found to be, you know, like, way beyond my talents.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:46.900
I mean, I was only able, I played through, like, maybe a half dozen games, and I only won once, and that was with using, I think I did two cancellations in that match.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:53.540
So, I mean, if we're going by the real rules of the game, I didn't win a single one, even on level one.

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:56.500
I don't know how you guys performed.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:01.680
I did, like, four games, and I think I won two, both against level one.

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:05.480
I played one against level four, and I don't think I won that one.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:20.600
I guess we'll start on just computer difficulty in games like Othello, because I do think that the computer difficulty in this game is making a mistake here in the way that it was programmed,

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:25.940
because there really needs to be, especially for the audience that this is designed for, right?

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:33.300
I doubt there were people, like, there are always people who are going to be, like, hardcore into chess or for the Eastern audience, like, hardcore into go.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:34.920
There's always going to be that.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:36.800
But this is not played at the same level.

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:42.940
And so a lot of people who are picking up this game need to, like, first learn how to play this game.

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:46.860
And there's no difficulty like that for the computer in this game.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:55.040
There's no, like, the computer will hand you victories, but you will also learn, like, why you were able to beat the computer,

00:21:55.040 --> 00:22:01.400
because you continue to take more than you give, and you figure out, like, oh, the corners are super important,

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:05.640
and I need to dominate, like, fixed spaces that the computer will never be able to get back.

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:12.660
Like, to learn those things is something that I had to go outside of the game to find out.

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:14.660
It was not something that the game was going to teach me here.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:22.260
And I'm not asking for tutorials, but I'm saying when the computer only really ever makes good to great to amazing moves,

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:31.340
we're really just missing out on the experience of ever, like, learning from something that might be able to teach us the game in a more intuitive way.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:32.480
I don't know, though. Without tutorials...

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:34.680
And that sounds like you're asking for a tutorial.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:40.540
Yeah, that's what I'm thinking, too, because I think without tutorials, the only way to do that would be...

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:45.580
Well, yeah, I mean, to do it the way that you're saying, where it's, like, just have it so the AI is more flawed,

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:49.000
so it's, like, an even easier mode would probably be fine,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:57.500
but I feel like playing against, like, a competent computer opponent still made me...

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.300
Like I said, I've played this when I was younger.

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.900
I haven't played it in years, so I had forgotten any strategy I had.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:03.720
And after a couple...

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:10.360
After my first two times playing, I was already kind of getting some strategy just from all the things I was doing wrong.

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:14.280
Like, I feel like it's the same thing as if you were to pick up...

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:18.220
You know, if you were to now go teach someone, or not teach someone, but play with someone who's a beginner

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:20.620
and you just start playing and you start making right moves,

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:26.200
unless you're going to verbally teach them, they'll just learn by losing to you, you know?

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:29.280
Or just seeing what's happening.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:30.400
I feel like it's just such a...

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:34.700
It's, like, you could say part of it could be trial and error, but also part of it is, like,

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:36.900
a lot of thinking ahead and, like...

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:41.520
It lends itself to you figuring out how to work it.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.300
Like, it's not like these techniques are impossible to figure out by thinking ahead.

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.760
Yeah, I think that's a great point.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:49.080
I believe in that, too.

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:53.500
And I heard, Sean, I think you were saying that it sounds like I kind of am asking for a tutorial,

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.020
which maybe I am, to be honest.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:57.800
Not sure exactly what I was looking for here.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:23:59.840
I would just say that, you know,

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:05.140
when you're playing against another person in a game like this,

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:10.860
either you fall into a trap where you're really good at this game from playing it all the time,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:14.560
and your friend isn't quite there and they have to, like, either catch up

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:19.100
or you just continue to learn how to exploit the common mistakes that they keep making.

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:20.500
Like when you have to play chess.

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:21.880
Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:26.980
Exactly. Or you have the computer who, you know,

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:32.140
because the only real thing it can do is just predict the consequences of the moves

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:34.960
that you're likely to make, just play better than you.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.440
I agree that, like, level one is not impossible,

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:44.760
but we have to mention that, like, the computer in this game is pretty damn good

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:50.520
and, like, there is no necessary, like, beginner mode for someone in this game.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.760
You will learn by losing to the computer even on level one.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.280
Like, you will learn from that, no doubt.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:25:04.140
Yeah. It's just not the same friendly video game experience that we might be, you know, used to today.

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:11.160
Yeah. I guess the closest thing that it offers is playing on the lowest difficulty level

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.000
also without a time limit.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.540
Because then you can...

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:19.620
Because, like I said, all the strategy is there before you.

00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:23.980
You just have to really be able to, like, take the time to really think about, like,

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:25.940
what's going to happen if I go here, what's going to happen if I go there?

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.620
You have to do what the AI is doing on level four.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.520
You have to sit there, probably take you a lot longer, and go through all your moves.

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:33.060
And, like, that's kind of how you...

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:38.440
Like, you can just, cognitively, you can just come up with those strategies

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:41.360
by figuring them out as you're looking at your next move.

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:43.020
So, like, I think...

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:44.760
I've never played this game.

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:48.680
I've never played Othello with a time limit, but I assume a lot of people do.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:51.140
And I think just being able to play it without a time limit

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:53.620
and without the pressure of having another person whose time you're wasting

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:57.060
gives you, like, just the infinite amount of time to, like, figure it out.

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.500
And then those strategies will come to you.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:03.960
It's not necessarily the cleanest solution to what you're asking for, Mike.

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:06.560
It's still just kind of like a workaround that the player has to do.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.280
But short of them having some sort of lessons or something.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.120
Yeah, that's the only thing that I can think of.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:15.500
I don't really know how they would go about it.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:18.940
Maybe have some baked-in situations that...

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:26.500
Sort of like when I use Lichess, like, every day they would have, like, a board

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.100
and arrange in a certain way.

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:29.660
What's the optimal move?

00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:35.560
And I guess just have, like, a dozen of these baked in to the game

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:40.380
that, like, once you select it, it'll say why that was the correct answer,

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:41.560
why that's the wrong answer.

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:47.320
But I don't know, like, especially in a game in this generation,

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.080
how in-depth they can go with that.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:59.200
I think that just an easy computer and a very generous time limit

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:01.840
is probably the best they can do beyond that.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.300
Yeah, that's a great point.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:10.620
And, you know, for what it's worth, we have talked about the game here,

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.560
and I think somewhat the strategy, but I would say that, you know,

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.360
even though Joe and I have familiar background with the game

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.900
and Sean seems to as well, I don't think any of us are experts enough

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:23.500
to tell you what are actually good strategies, just what our strategies are.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:27.520
But there's other things to talk about here, too.

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:29.500
And Joe mentioned the timers.

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:32.520
I don't know if this was just me, and maybe, like,

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:34.640
if you play against a level 4 computer often enough,

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:37.520
you would want the much longer timers.

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.020
But I was actually looking for a timer that was more like,

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:42.560
give me five minutes, you know?

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:48.080
Or do, like, do a chess clock style where, you know,

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:51.880
it's like bullet chess where you get a minute on each side,

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:55.840
but when you make a move, you gain 10 seconds of time.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:59.700
So you're kind of, like, always trying to make a move in 10 seconds

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:02.580
to gain more time, so it's where you have more time later

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:03.700
when you really need to think.

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:06.620
Like, you know, something like that where you gain time

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:08.540
for making fast decisions is interesting.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.560
I don't even know if the NES would be possible for that,

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:13.980
but I just thought the time thing was a little strange,

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:16.140
and I get that, like, the computer takes more time to think

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:17.860
in later ones and might need more time,

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:19.760
or you might need more time against it.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:22.260
But I was looking for, like, a shorter option, too.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:25.120
I mean, I agree that I didn't never,

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:26.900
I never really needed the 20 minutes.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:31.000
Maybe it's just because I was being too slapdash

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.260
with my decision-making, but I was,

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:37.700
it never felt too hard, even when I was at the 20-minute mark.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.940
But I feel like I'm getting deja vu.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:44.900
